<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franco-Savoyard War (1600–1601)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1600">1600</a>–<a href="/years/1601">1601</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Franco-Savoyard War of 1600–1601 was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-i-duke-of-savoy">Charles Emmanuel I, Duke of Savoy</a>, over the Marquisate of Saluzzo, which Savoy had seized in 1588. French armies quickly overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a> and Savoy. By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> of 1601 the duke kept Saluzzo but ceded Bresse, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugey">Bugey</a>, Valromey and Gex to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franco-savoyard-war-1600-1601.jpg" alt="Franco-Savoyard War (1600–1601)" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonyme (1er quart 17e siècle) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henri_IV_et_la_guerre_de_Savoie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Saluzzo, in the Piedmontese Alps, had been a French enclave since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> annexed it after the death of its last marquis, Gian Gabriele I, in 1548. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s claim was relatively weak, and Charles Emmanuel I, pursuing the expansion of his duchy, occupied the marquisate in the autumn of 1588, while France was weakened by civil war, on the pretext of keeping it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> of Dauphiné.</p><p>In 1595 Henry IV offered Saluzzo to one of the duke's sons as a French fief, but Charles Emmanuel wanted it outright. Papal arbitration failed. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> in December 1599 Henry offered two choices: return Saluzzo, or keep it and cede the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a>, the vicarship of Barcelonnette and the Stura, Perosa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinerolo">Pinerolo</a> valleys. The duke went home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, prepared for war and hoped for Spanish help from the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, the Count of Fuentes. After an ultimatum, Henry declared war in August 1600.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Charles de Gontaut, Duke of Biron, took Bourg-en-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a>, though not its citadel, and occupied most of Bresse, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugey">Bugey</a> and Gex. Charles de Blanchefort, stepson of the Duke of Lesdiguières, took Montmélian; Chambery opened its gates, and Lesdiguières took the fortress of Miolans and Conflans. The French then occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurienne">Maurienne</a> and Tarentaise valleys, though a move against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> by Charles, Duke of Guise, was beaten off. In early October Henry entered Annecy.</p><p>In November Charles Emmanuel marched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta-valley">Aosta Valley</a> with some twenty thousand Piedmontese, Spaniards, Swiss and Savoyards but made no headway. The citadel of Montmélian surrendered in mid-November, and a month later a French and Genevese force took and demolished the fortress of Sainte Catherine, built to threaten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. By the end of the year only the citadel of Bourg still held out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had lost little, but the threat of Spanish intervention led Henry to accept peace, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> was signed on 17 January 1601. Besides the lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, Savoy paid France 300,000 livres, and in exchange France gave up its Italian holdings beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. The Spanish Road linking Franche-Comté with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> was kept open by a new route over the Bridge of Grésin, confined to a single valley, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> objected to a route through the territory of Bern.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Franco-Savoyard War (1600–1601)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
